[INFO] 10-01 17:18:30 execution.py:378] In computational complexity theory, a set \( S \subseteq \Sigma^* \) is defined as **sparse** if there exists a polynomial \( p : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N} \) such that for every \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), the number of strings of length \( n \) in \( S \) is at most \( p(n) \). Formally, if we denote by \( S^{=n} \) the subset of \( S \) consisting of all strings in \( S \) that have length \( n \), the sparse set condition can be expressed as:

\[
|S^{=n}| \leq p(n)
\]

This means that the growth rate of the number of elements in \( S \) of any given length \( n \) is bounded by some polynomial function of \( n \), ensuring that \( S \) does not become too "dense" as the length of strings increases.
